From c59b85e104246513818515e2f351e887d380cc04 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: William Floyd <git@notmy.space>
Date: Sun, 27 Sep 2026 04:41:44 +0000
Subject: ipa: softisp: awb: Take the colour temperature from the sensor's
 locus

The colour temperature Ccm and Lsc select their tables by comes from
estimateCCT(), whose RGB to XYZ matrix is a fixed one, not the
sensor's: fed the white points a phone module's vendor tuning gives
for its illuminants, it reads Horizon (2300 K) as 2725 K and A
(2850 K) as 3291 K, the range where lens shading changes most.

Read the sensor's white points as colourGains, the key libipa's
AwbGrey uses for its colour temperature curve: the gains that make
each colour temperature's illuminant grey. With them, the colour
temperature is that of the nearest point on the polyline through
the white points, in R/G and B/G, interpolated in mired along the
segment. The gains are grey world as before; without colourGains
nothing changes.

Grey world makes the mean of the image grey, so a scene that is
mostly one colour, grass or a painted wall, pulls the gains towards
its complement and tints the whole image. The sensor's locus says
where real illuminants lie.

When the tuning has colourGains, move the grey world estimate, in
R/G and B/G, to within transversePos (towards purple) and
transverseNeg (towards green) of its nearest point on the locus,
the keys and defaults AwbBayes uses, and derive the gains from
that. Estimates beyond the locus's ends are held at the same
distance from them. Without colourGains nothing changes.

With the black level exact, a frame of almost nothing, a covered lens
or a dark room, sums to close to 0 above black in every channel, and
grey world's ratios are then noise: a lens-down phone came out navy
under one run and magenta under the next.

Keep the previous gains while the mean green is under 2.5 (8-bit, 1%
of the range) above black. With a locus, start from its 5000 K point
rather than from the sensor's own colour, so that a camera opened in
the dark does not stay green.

A sensor's illuminants off its daylight locus are fluorescent tubes,
at around 3800 to 4100 K, but the band grey world is kept within was
one width at every colour temperature: as wide as those tubes need
everywhere. Under a warm lamp, a scene with much green in it then
pulled grey world 0.03 towards green, and the image came out green.

Accept transversePos and transverseNeg as piecewise linear functions
of the colour temperature as well as widths, evaluated at the fit's
colour temperature.

Grey world, kept to the sensor's locus, neutralises the light it
finds, warm lamps included, which makes a room under 2700 K lamps look
cold, and a little green where its colours pull grey world. Phone
tunings keep some of the light's warmth: Qualcomm's (triglGAV1) with
factors on the red and blue gains over a mesh of white points and by
colour temperature.

Accept a gainAdjust in the tuning: the mesh's vertices (white point,
red and blue factors) and triangles, and optional factors by colour
temperature. The mesh's factors are interpolated across the triangle
the fitted white point falls in, multiplied by the colour
temperature's, and applied to the red and blue gains.

